										<content:encoded><![CDATA[
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Ever wondered how Passive Houses stay comfy year-round? Maintaining consistent internal temperature so homes are warm in winter and cool in summer?</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Well, it’s all in the design! From super installation and airtight construction to high performance windows and ventilation with heat recovery, the features of a passive house are carefully selected and designed retaining their top standard for ultra-efficient, comfortable, and low-energy homes.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Unlike typical houses, Passive Houses focus on reducing energy demand rather than relying solely on renewable energy systems to compensate for heat loss or inefficiency. The goal is simple, to create a building so efficient that it barely needs heating or cooling in the first place.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">This high level of performance is achieved through a combination of smart design, precision construction, and a commitment to sustainability. At the core of every Passive House are five key principles, each working together to create exceptional comfort, health, and efficiency:</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Super-Insulation</strong></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Passive Houses feature walls, roofs, and floors that are insulated far beyond standard building regulations. This thick, continuous insulation layer keeps the heat where you want it, dramatically reducing heat loss, helping to keep the inside warm during winter and cool during summer. The result? A stable indoor temperature with minimal need for active heating or cooling.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Airtight Construction</strong></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A key feature of Passive Houses is their extremely airtight building design. Precision construction techniques, specialised tapes, and membranes ensure that unwanted drafts are eliminated and conditioned air stays inside. By preventing heat from leaking out (and unconditioned air from leaking in), energy waste is significantly reduced, and indoor comfort is improved.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>High-Performance Windows</strong></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Windows are one of the biggest areas of heat loss in traditional homes, which is why Passive Houses use triple-glazed, low-emissivity windows. These high-performance windows allow sunlight to enter while keeping heat where you want it, inside during winter and outside during summer. They also help minimise cold spots and draughts around window areas.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Ventilation with Heat Recovery</strong></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">To maintain fresh indoor air without sacrificing heat, Passive Houses use a mechanical ventilation system with heat recovery (MVHR). This system continuously supplies filtered, fresh air while extracting the stale air from inside the home. It transfers the heat from the outgoing air to the incoming air, ensuring excellent air quality with minimal energy loss. What more could you ask for!?</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Thermal Bridge-Free Design</strong></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Thermal bridges are areas where heat can easily escape, (corners, weak spots, structural connections) which are covered up through thoughtful detailing. By designing every joint carefully and avoiding weak points in the building design, Passive Houses maintain consistent indoor temperatures and prevent condensation or cold spots from forming.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Passive House Tradesperson</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Tim Day is a certified Passive House tradesperson, but what does that mean? A Passive House tradesperson is a skilled contractor or craftsperson who specialises in building or retrofitting homes to Passive House standards.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Unlike standard tradespeople, they have expertise in the high-precision construction techniques, materials, and systems required to achieve ultra-efficient, low-energy buildings. Without their expertise, even the best-designed low-carbon homes may fail to deliver the efficiency and comfort that Net Zero and the Future Homes Standard demand.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Whether you’re planning a new project or looking to upgrade an existing home, embracing Passive House principles can help you create a home that’s built for the future.</p>



